        "excerpt": "FIGURE 4.45 Sessile serrated adenoma, resection. This is a section of a large lesion that extends for nearly the entire span of the mucosa. This image demonstrates how subtle these polyps can be for endoscopy colleagues to recognize since the lesion follows the contours of the mucosa rather than forming a protuberance. Even at this magnification (original magnification\u20141\u00d7), it is apparent that the bases of some of the crypts are dilated. However, many of the crypts are narrow-based. If a small biopsy is obtained from an area with narrow crypts, it is easy to mistake the process for a hyperplastic polyp. As such, it is often helpful to correlate with endoscopic findings before diagnosing a serra",
        "text": "FIGURE 4.45 Sessile serrated adenoma, resection. This is a section of a large lesion that extends for nearly the entire span of the mucosa. This image demonstrates how subtle these polyps can be for endoscopy colleagues to recognize since the lesion follows the contours of the mucosa rather than forming a protuberance. Even at this magnification (original magnification\u20141\u00d7), it is apparent that the bases of some of the crypts are dilated. However, many of the crypts are narrow-based. If a small biopsy is obtained from an area with narrow crypts, it is easy to mistake the process for a hyperplastic polyp. As such, it is often helpful to correlate with endoscopic findings before diagnosing a serra",
